WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.280
Bootstrapping a pure Swift application yields a clean, linear environment that compiles almost instantly.

2
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:12.880
Generating a React Native iOS project produces an entirely different structure.

3
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:19.320
Running the initialization command generates an iOS directory packed with specific configuration files.

4
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:26.640
Most notably, it produces both an Xcode project file and a CocoaPods workspace file, sitting right next to each other.

5
00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:33.120
Modern iOS developers are trained to reach for Swift package manager and pure.swift entry points.

6
00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:39.040
They expect to manage dependencies and application logic natively through Apple's first party tool chain.

7
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:45.440
Forcing those standard tools onto a React Native code base leads directly to day one build failures.

8
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.560
The compiler will immediately throw missing module errors and refuse to build the app.

9
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.560
React Native's underlying architecture requires this specific tooling

10
00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:00.160
because its core is built in C++ and Objective C++ rather than pure Swift.

11
00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.520
Treating these CocoaCods and workspace requirements as structural necessities is the baseline for

12
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:10.960
preventing the build errors we're about to deconstruct. Inside the generated iOS folder,

13
00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.440
you are presented with two distinct options to open the completely new code base,

14
00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:23.280
MyApp.xcode project and MyApp.xcode workspace. This diagram maps the Xcode project boundaries.

15
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.680
It isolates application targets without awareness of external dependencies.

16
00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:33.440
Opening the project file fails immediately with a no such module React error, lacking a dependency map.

17
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.480
The workspace file physically links your main target with the CocoaPods project.

18
00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:41.440
With the workspace open, the compiler resolves paths.

19
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.960
It maps your application code to the necessary react native modules,

20
00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:50.480
sitting in the adjacent pod's target. The Xcode workspace file is the mandatory structural glue

21
00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:55.200
required to compile native dependencies alongside your code. Inside the workspace,

22
00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:02.320
the initialization entry point is app.mm. The .mm extension denotes Objective C++,

23
00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:08.080
rather than the standard .swift extension used in modern iOS apps. This maps the react native

24
00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:14.960
runtime. JSI, fabric, and turbo modules are heavy C++ components emitting C++ headers.

25
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:20.000
A .swift file cannot interact directly. Objective C++ translates.

26
00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.640
AppDelegate.mm imports these headers, bootstrapping the runtime,

27
00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.520
and exposing a clean upward interface for your Swift code.

28
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.200
If you need to execute Swift logic during the initial bootstrap,

29
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.520
you can route a helper file through a bridging header,

30
00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:37.840
but the entry file executing the startup sequence must stay in Objective C++.

31
00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.960
The .mm entry point is a rigid boundary isolating the C++ runtime.

32
00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.880
Relying on this C++ architecture heavily restricts how dependencies are managed.

33
00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:53.600
This matrix compares Coco Pods to SPM. SPM lacks mechanisms for complex C++ flags.

34
00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.720
Coco Pods uses pod target X config to inject C++ requirements directly into the compiler,

35
00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:05.200
a future SPM lacks entirely. Additionally, the react native post-install macro sweeps across

36
00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:12.640
resolved pods, standardizing build settings uniformly in one pass. Furthermore, Coco Pods utilizes

37
00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.240
the use native modules macro to scan your node modules directory, automatically detecting and

38
00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:25.600
linking the native code for any installed NPM package. React native 0.76 ships experimental SPM

39
00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.800
support for a small subset of core packages, but compiling the full runtime stack,

40
00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.120
turbo modules, fabric, and hermys still demands Coco Pods completely.

41
00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.560
You can still add pure Swift utility libraries through X codes native SPM integration.

42
00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:44.080
The two package managers can coexist in the same project.

43
00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:49.200
For the react native full stack, Coco Pods is a first class structural requirement.

44
00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:53.600
Bypassing it completely breaks the C++ tool chain and halts builds.

45
00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:57.520
Because the build depends entirely on these linked packages,

46
00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.400
the exact version of every dependency must be strictly enforced across all environments.

47
00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:08.240
This flow mapping shows version control. Running pod install evaluates your project

48
00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:14.000
and pins dependencies to the exact version in the lock file. Running pod update breaks that lock,

49
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:19.200
deliberately seeking newer, potentially breaking versions. Use it only when actively upgrading

50
00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:25.360
react native. The pod file dot lock is the single source of truth for the entire native iOS layer.

51
00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:30.320
It must absolutely always be committed to your version control system to ensure all developers run

52
00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:35.840
the exact same code. In contrast, the massive pods directory should be ignored by version control.

53
00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:42.000
Because the lock file is perfectly deterministic, the entire directory can be reproduced flawlessly.

54
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.360
If your project includes a gem file to manage Ruby environments,

55
00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:50.240
you must run the bundle exact pod install command to maintain absolute control.

56
00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:54.240
This forces the system to use the local isolated version of Coco Pods,

57
00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:57.200
avoiding conflicts with globally installed versions on a CI server.

58
00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.320
Strict adherence to the lock file and perfectly localized Ruby environments

59
00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:08.560
eliminates the works on my machine class of build divergence. This pipeline traces the sequence.

60
00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.800
NPM installs dependencies, Coco Pods links native code,

61
00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:19.200
and the runtime is bridged through objective C++. For a no such module react error,

62
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:26.400
you opened .xc project instead of .xc workspace. For undefined symbols, you installed the NPM

63
00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:31.840
module but skipped pod install. If you hit a non-modular header error, trace it back to the

64
00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:38.320
dependency configuration. The required react native post install block is missing from your pod file,

65
00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:44.880
preventing the compiler from applying the necessary C++ flags. Internalizing these C++ constraints and

66
00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:50.880
the Coco Pods sequence makes the underlying iOS build tooling a predictable invisible part of the

67
00:05:50.880 --> 00:06:00.880
development cycle.

